{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"duration_ms":89922,"duration_api_ms":140654,"num_turns":7,"result":"","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"1ed1fd50-9def-4847-9811-e155eb1195c3","total_cost_usd":0.5068237500000001,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":40892,"cache_read_input_tokens":70319,"output_tokens":2689,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":40892,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":60,"outputTokens":8065,"cacheReadInputTokens":181430,"cacheCreationInputTokens":72253,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.14884424999999998,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000},"claude-opus-4-6":{"inputTokens":4,"outputTokens":2689,"cacheReadInputTokens":70319,"cacheCreationInputTokens":40892,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.35797949999999995,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":64000}},"permission_denials":[],"structured_output":{"summary":"All five amendments address real mass-conservation or correctness bugs in the tank simulation. After examining the source code for each, I find them all technically sound and warranting acceptance. The most critical are fr-4-911c8bce (loss routing exceeding available biomass creates mass from nothing) and fr-4-12ae787f (NOB growth using wrong substrate). The documentation amendment fr-4-74c76994 is lower severity but still valuable for maintainability.","votes":[{"amendment_id":"fr-4-4aff0bdf","decision":"accept","rationale":"Confirmed in algae_growth.rs:200-204: the `.min(periphyton_capacity_g)` cap silently discards biomass when the uncapped value exceeds capacity. The excess N/C is not routed anywhere — it simply vanishes. This is a genuine mass-conservation violation. The proposed fix (compute excess before capping, route it via route_algae_loss_to_dissolved_organics) is straightforward and correct."},{"amendment_id":"fr-4-00ebb97d","decision":"accept","rationale":"Confirmed in engine.rs:353-366: CleanFilter multiplies four microbe biomass pools by (1.0 - setback) without routing removed mass anywhere. The budget guard system exempts clean_filter from nitrogen validation (tick_has_closed_system_nitrogen returns false), which suggests the intent may be physical removal (down the drain), but this is not documented and there is no explicit export entry. The amendment's suggestion to either route to detritus/dissolved organics OR document the export and add an explicit budget entry is valid — either approach fixes the ambiguity and makes the mass flow auditable."},{"amendment_id":"fr-4-74c76994","decision":"accept","rationale":"Confirmed: neither nitrogen_budget_components nor carbon_budget_components has any doc comments. The test relies on naming conventions (_mg_n_total, _mg_c_total, biomass_g) that are not documented anywhere at the function declaration site. A future developer adding a field with a non-standard suffix (e.g., organic_matter_g) would break mass tracking silently. Adding doc comments explaining the convention and the obligation to update is low-effort, low-risk, and directly improves maintainability of a critical safety mechanism."},{"amendment_id":"fr-4-12ae787f","decision":"accept","rationale":"Confirmed two distinct bugs in nitrogen_cycle.rs:286-324. (1) AOB/NOB/comammox growth calls constrained_live_growth_g with state.water.ammonia_total_mg_n_total AFTER TAN has been debited for oxidation. Since consume_live_growth_inputs draws from that same post-debit pool, growth collapses to zero whenever oxidation fully consumes TAN — even when nitrification was successful and growth_yield is positive. (2) NOB growth (lines 301-308) feeds from ammonia_total_mg_n_total, but NOB oxidizes nitrite, not ammonia. NOB's N source should be the nitrite it just processed. Both issues are clear biochemical modeling errors that would produce incorrect nitrifier dynamics."},{"amendment_id":"fr-4-911c8bce","decision":"accept","rationale":"Confirmed in both plant_growth.rs:151-157 and algae_growth.rs:116-125,200-209. The code clamps biomass to zero with .max(0.0) but routes the full unclamped loss amounts (respiration_g, grazing_g, senescence_g) to dissolved organics and detritus. When losses exceed available biomass, more mass is routed out than actually existed, creating nitrogen and carbon from nothing. This is a direct mass-conservation violation. The fix — clamp routed losses to (before_biomass - after_biomass) — is correct and necessary."}]},"fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"e887a28a-aafb-42e6-a08d-d359efd4fdb2"}
